{ignore}
<p><code>{sandbox}...{/sandbox}</code> does what <a href="?tag/code">{code}</a> does - renders
its content as PAD source in a separate engine pass - with the pass sandboxed. It is
<code>{code sandbox}</code> under a name of its own.</p>

<p>Sandboxed means: going in, the page's variables are hidden and the data, content, bool and
sequence stores are emptied; coming out, everything is restored and what the pass set is gone.
A field of the page is missing inside - write <code>{echo $city ?? 'default'}</code> when a
snippet may run either way - and a <code>{push}</code> made inside fills the pass's own stacks,
not the page's.</p>

<p>The pipe form, <code>{echo $snippet | sandbox}</code>, runs a value as a template in the
same isolation. It is the safer choice for template text that comes from outside the code -
an editor, a database - when that text should not read or change the page's state. It is not a
security boundary for untrusted authors on its own: the template language can still call what
<code>$padPhpFunctions</code> allows.</p>
{/ignore}
